      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.089,
            0.847,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 1.2, the equalities are between two angles and the non-included side of the two triangles. This condition is referred to as the **AAS (Angle Angle Side)** condition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.147,
            0.759,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": "As we have seen, the AAS condition guarantees congruence."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.166,
            0.848,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that the SSA condition doesn’t always guarantee congruence. However, there are some special cases when SSA does guarantee congruence. Here is one such important case."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.241,
            0.648,
            0.264
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Measuring Two Sides in a Right Triangle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.266,
            0.846,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "？ ΔABC and ΔXYZ are right-angled triangles such that $BC = YZ = 4$ cm, $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{Y} = 90^{\\circ}$ and AC = XZ=5cm. Are they congruent?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.327,
            0.847,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "？ Can there exist non-congruent triangles having these measurements? Construct and find out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.39,
            0.787,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": "Looking at the rough diagram helps in planning the construction."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.419,
            0.625,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.59,
            0.549,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Draw the base QR of length 4 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.608,
            0.634,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Draw a line l perpendicular to QR from Q."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.628,
            0.639,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** From R, cut an arc on line l of radius 5 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.646,
            0.832,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** Let P be the point at which the arc intersects the line l. Join PR."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.671,
            0.627,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.862,
            0.445,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": "ΔPQR is the required triangle."
        },
